<Fragment>
  <!--
    Page geometry — A4 portrait, 794 x 1123 px at 96 dpi (210 x 297 mm).

      margin  56 left / right          content width 682
      top     64 (masthead sits above, in the master)
      bottom  74 (folio sits below, in the master)

    One rule exists in this document: the hairline that closes the running
    head. Columns are separated by the gutter, not by a line — where the
    column holds cards, the card borders already divide it, and where it holds
    prose, the gutter does. Ticks, dots, accent stubs, per-row underlines and
    column rules are all a line drawn where nothing needed dividing.

    Never set these numbers on a slide. Every page goes through a page
    template, and the template owns the padding.
  -->
  <Styles>
    <Style name="fill" h="max" w="100%" />

    <!-- The only rule -->
    <Style name="hairline" backgroundColor="E2E5EA" h="1" w="100%" />

    <!--
      One box, and it is the running head's meta block.

      A tinted box around an aside, and an ink mat around a figure that
      already carries its own dark background, are both a second frame drawn
      over content that was already bounded. An aside is marked by one rule
      above it and a heading; a figure is bounded by its own edge.

      The exception is `metabox`: two label/value rows that have to line up
      with each other and stay apart from the chapter path beside them. A
      tint does that with one element where alignment alone would not.
    -->
    <Style name="metabox" backgroundColor="F5F6F8" padding="12 16" borderRadius="1" />

    <!--
      Requirement badge. A requirement number inside the page body is a
      citation, not a sentence, and left as bare text under a heading it reads
      as a stray fragment. The chip is the second exception to the rule above:
      it bounds a token, not a block of content. It carries the accent at the
      lightest tint that still separates from paper, so a page can hold several
      without the page turning blue. The running head never uses it — there the
      meta box already does the bounding.
    -->
    <Style name="reqbadge" backgroundColor="EEF2FA" borderRadius="2"
                           border.color="C6D5EC" border.width="0.75"
                           padding="2 7" h="13" />

    <!--
      Cards. A discrete item — one requirement, one device, one stage — has an
      edge in the reader's head, and a card is that edge drawn. The rule
      against boxes above is about a frame drawn over content that was already
      bounded; a list of separate items is not that.

      One border weight, one radius, one title-bar tint. A card never sits
      inside another card.
    -->
    <Style name="card"       backgroundColor="FFFFFF" borderRadius="2"
                             border.color="E2E5EA" border.width="1" />
    <Style name="card-bar"   backgroundColor="F5F6F8" padding="11 17" w="100%" />
    <Style name="card-bar-ink" backgroundColor="14161C" padding="11 17" w="100%" />
    <Style name="card-body"  padding="16 17" w="100%" />

    <!--
      Title bars. A bar is the heading of a whole region — it spans the text
      block and closes the section above it. The plain icon heading (`h2`)
      stays the sub-level inside a region, so the two never compete: one bar
      per region, headings beneath it.

      The filled bars carry a 부 colour, so they live in the generated
      styles/parts.xml with the rest of the palette. This one does not fill
      anything: it opens a region with a rule alone.
    -->
    <Style name="bar-quiet" padding="0 0 9 0" w="100%"
                            borderBottom.color="14161C" borderBottom.width="2" />
  </Styles>
</Fragment>
